# Sweep databases and generated reports. Globbed rather than listed one by one:
# five ad-hoc names accumulated during measurement and each new one had to be
# remembered, which is how eval-budget-smoke.db and report-control-final.html ended
# up untracked-but-visible in a portability commit. report.html is the ONE committed
# report (the recorded finding) so it is force-included below.
#
# The six databases behind the published 121-run result are force-included TOO, and
# deliberately: with them ignored, a fresh clone could read "121 runs for $0.353,
# twelve judged cheats, zero tampering" and had no way to check any of it — not the
# totals, not a judge verdict, not one trajectory. They are listed by name rather
# than un-globbed, so a NEW ad-hoc sweep database is still ignored by default and
# only becomes evidence when someone adds it here and to RECORDED in src/evidence.ts,
# which `npm run evidence` then holds to the published number.
# WAL files stay ignored: checkpoint before committing, or the .db is incomplete.
eval*.db
eval*.db-wal
eval*.db-shm
smoke*.db*
report-*.html
!report.html
!eval.db
!eval-hard.db
!eval-control.db
!eval-judge.db
!eval-control-final-retry.db
!eval-budget-smoke.db
# Recorded 2026-08-19 under the rev-6 schema: UNIQUE (run_id, seq), the re-run archive,
# read-only reads. These are the first sweeps whose trajectories are structurally
# unambiguous and whose re-run history is recorded rather than unknowable.
!eval-easy-r3.db
!eval-hard-r5.db
!eval-control-judge-r3.db
